Hi Robin,

Fascinating paper on commuting in Netherlands (can't access the full versio=
n unfortunately), basically modelling the opposite of what is normally done=
 in countries trying to demonstrate benefits from cycling, to show what wou=
ld happen without it? Some forward thinking assumptions on valuation of tim=
e, i.e. assuming hourly rate of employee, which makes a lot of sense. Is qu=
ite common for people to be paid for their commute over here, around 20c a =
km for cycling and driving. I had a phase of riding to the Hague office and=
 bagged =E2=82=AC12 a trip (but then had to pay =E2=82=AC7.50 to put bike o=
n the train on the way home!) but if it was my hourly salary the numbers wo=
uld have looked a lot different (but then driving would be 'cheaper' for th=
e employer for long distances?).

The 2007 travel data work is really interesting, although the large assumpt=
ions needed due to data gaps always scuppers my motivation somewhat, but yo=
u mention there is a lot more data which could be exciting. Is this data ap=
plied to

I would also be keen to apply this work to the logistics industry which is =
not just critical for shops but increasingly for private customers. Yet roa=
d haulage is still considered to be the cheapest option despite enormous sy=
stems costs, for example around 1/5th of motorway traffic is HGVs but 1/3 o=
f all fatal motorway collisions involve a HGV. They use taxpayer funded roa=
ds for free whereas rail haulage have to pay significant rental fees for th=
e rails. The energy cost of pushing individual trucks through the air is en=
ormous compared with single file on a train and truck drivers have one of t=
he lowest life expectancies of any profession and are away from their commu=
nities for long periods.

Putting long distance freight on rail and having more people riding cargo b=
ikes for "last mile" deliveries could mean those drivers have jobs in their=
 hometowns doing something active. The bike messenger community is really s=
trong (although also because it is niche, so might not scale) and there is =
nothing like this in the "white van man" world.

One of my career regrets is working on the rail freight interchange in Hinc=
kely between 2017and 2018. The biggest impasse was the effect of increased =
traffic levels in the area, most of which was commute traffic to the site f=
or all the workers. The financial benefits of the project for the developer=
 were enormous, but the solutions on the table from the transport consultan=
t were all about increasing road capacity to deal with this, which was unac=
ceptable to the local community and for the aq limit values. Imagine if the=
y had instead decided to build a segregated cycle path along every road and=
 give every employee an ebike as part of their contract? I was so caught up=
 in how things were done and quite inexperienced that I didn't dare suggest=
, but the project still hasn't been approved.

However, the topic I am really keen on is the air quality (and systems) ben=
efits of reallocating urban space. I recently spotted this paper https://ww=
w.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2214140526000654 which Rosa Felix =
was involved in. Is interesting it is stated that this is something that is=
 not really focused on in the air quality world, which is my experience als=
o.

After attending the R course run by David Carslaw and Stuart Grange in 2018=
 I applied their meteorological normalisation random forest model (Stuarts =
PhD) to the then most polluted street in the UK, Upper Thames Street in Lon=
don, and noticed a >20% drop in roadside NO2 concentrations at around the t=
ime construction of the cycle superhighway began (i.e. road space reduced) =
https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/arcticle-blaise-kelly. This is larger and mu=
ch faster than the reductions from the Clean Air Zones in many parts of the=
 country that were introduced (apparently the fastest route to compliance, =
but actually the only scenario modelled) but have taken more than 5 years t=
o make any significant impact (at the recent select committee a 3% NO2 redu=
ction in the West Midlands was hailed as =E2=80=9Cstatistically significant=
=E2=80=9D). I recently visually inspected all ~350 roadside aq sites (over =
a few weeks whenever I was in front of the TV) on Google street view to loo=
k for significant road space changes, particularly COVID popup cycle paths =
were installed. I think there is something there but unfortunately so few s=
ites had lanes (<10) and even fewer on the side of the road where the monit=
oring site was, it is maybe hard to make a robust analysis and is still a w=
ork in progress.

Similarly the effect of 20mph on air pollution is difficult to quantify bec=
ause they are poorly enforced (the only average speed camera 20mph section =
I know of is Tower Bridge) and studies with test drivers have biases. But s=
omeone adhering rigorously to 20 drives far less aggressively. 20mph is als=
o the most energy efficient speed for EVs, even on a motorway.

Have a lot of experience using the ECMWF ERA5 meteo data which has 7-30km2 =
resolution modelled data for more than 200 meteo parameters hourly stretchi=
ng from last month to 1950. Have mainly been using this for air quality mod=
els (boundary layer/mixing height etc), but I also demonstrated how it coul=
d be used to calculate WBGT which we combined with air pollution and pollen=
 to come up with a stress model that can be applied across Europe. But ther=
e is also potential to be used for e.g. collision data (basic weather data/=
road collision is recorded).

I mentioned I was using the free allowance of the Google Distance Matrix AP=
I requesting time travelled for the next week, which I think represents the=
 current week. I put this analysis together for Wiltshire Council https://b=
laisekelly.github.io/B3108_speeds/journey_time.html. I am not sure what wei=
ght it had but they have been successful in reducing the limit, despite bei=
ng originally told there were not enough fatalities.

There have been some questions over whether it is allowed to use Google dat=
a in this way (from Dustin and others). Yet a core part of my former collea=
gue Tilman Hohenberger=E2=80=99 PhD was scraping google map data for the co=
lour coded congestion data (i.e. by scripting the opening of google maps in=
 a browser, screenshotting and extracting the colours   (https://ui.adsabs.=
harvard.edu/abs/2023AGUFMGC13C..07L/abstract) which uses no API and is almo=
st certainly against its terms and conditions. Tilman used this tool to dev=
elop an emissions model that better considers congestion across Europe for =
TNO which they have also published (without actually mentioning Google!) ht=
tps://essd.copernicus.org/articles/18/3959/2026/

I don't really think Google ar bothered because they don=E2=80=99t offer th=
is data at all (apart from via the Distance Matrix) and it is my understand=
ing their T&Cs are more focused on people copying Google maps rather than p=
eople using Google maps data and actually promoting their product.
Not a PhD topic but the ability to get congestion data for anywhere is usef=
ul and I would like to work more on developing these tools, maybe using sat=
ellite data etc.

Apologies for the length of this. I wrote more and tried to trim it down.

Speak later!

Blaise





